{
  "image_name": "0eb8b22b5c668764a54faeffdd396acc.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares several proposed charge-transfer pathways in two-semiconductor photocatalytic systems and emphasizes why some common models are problematic. In all schemes, light excites electrons from the valence band to the conduction band in two particles, labeled P1 and P2.\n\nThe top row contrasts a conventional type-II heterojunction with a liquid-phase Z-scheme. In the type-II picture, electrons and holes are expected to separate across the two materials, but the diagram highlights electrostatic repulsion and notes reduced redox ability and bandgap limitations. In the liquid-phase Z-scheme, a redox mediator such as Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ is shown between the particles, but the figure marks the transfer directions as thermodynamically or dynamically unfavorable, suggesting the mediator-based route is difficult to realize in freely dispersed suspensions.\n\nThe bottom row presents the key distinction between a photoelectrochemical cell and particulate photocatalysis. In a cell with electrodes and an external circuit, charge flow is directed and collected, making spatial separation feasible. In contrast, for suspended photocatalyst particles without an external circuit, the inset indicates that the favorable process is recombination of the less reactive carriers, leaving the stronger oxidizing holes and stronger reducing electrons on opposite semiconductors. Overall, the figure argues for direct Z-scheme behavior rather than type-II or liquid mediator transfer in photocatalysis.",
